            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><strong>{most_covered}</strong> has the widest coverage ({cov_counts[most_covered]:,} stations, {cov_counts[most_covered]/total_stations*100:.1f}% of all stations), while <strong>{least_covered}</strong> is available at only {cov_counts[least_covered]:,} stations ({cov_counts[least_covered]/total_stations*100:.1f}%).</p>

        <p>On average, each station reports <strong>{fuel_per_station.mean():.1f}</strong> fuel types. <strong>{stations_one_only:,} stations</strong> report only a single fuel type, while <strong>{stations_all_four:,}</strong> report all fuel types present in the dataset. This uneven coverage means price comparisons across all fuel types are not possible for a substantial number of stations.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════ Section 6: Daily Trend ══════ -->
<section>
    <div class="section-header">
        <h2>6. Daily Price Trends</h2>
        <p class="objective">Daily average fuel prices across February 2026 to identify any intra-month trends.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
        <div class="chart-container">{charts['price_timeseries']}</div>
        <p>The daily price chart shows relatively stable average prices across February 2026, with minor day-to-day fluctuations driven by station-level price changes. No sharp spikes or drops are visible, indicating a broadly stable pricing environment during this period.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- ══════ Section 7: Key Findings ══════ -->
<section>
    <div class="section-header">
        <h2>7. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
        <p class="objective">Synthesised insights for fuel pricing policy, consumer protection, and market transparency.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="section-body">
        <ol class="findings-list">
            <li>
                <strong>Price Hierarchy Aligned with Market Structure.</strong> Premium fuels (E5, B7_PREMIUM) command {fmt_pence(price_stats.loc['E5','mean'])} – {fmt_pence(price_stats.loc['B7_PREMIUM','mean'])} per litre on average, while standard grades (E10, B7_STANDARD) average {fmt_pence(price_stats.loc['E10','mean'])} – {fmt_pence(price_stats.loc['B7_STANDARD','mean'])}. This differential is consistent with the higher production costs of premium grades and does not, in itself, indicate a competition problem.
            </li>
            <li class="high">
                <strong>Significant Price Dispersion Raises Consumer Protection Questions.</strong> The {biggest_spread} price spread of <strong>{biggest_spread_val:.1f}p/litre</strong> across stations translates to a £{(biggest_spread_val / 100 * 55):.2f} difference per 55-litre fill — a material amount for consumers. The IQR of {biggest_iqr_val:.1f}p indicates that even the typical consumer faces non-trivial price variation. Policy-makers should consider whether this dispersion reflects genuine competitive differentiation or information asymmetry.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Update Freshness Is Uneven and a Compliance Concern.</strong> The median update lag varies from {best_lag_val:.1f}h ({best_lag_fuel}) to {worst_lag_val:.1f}h ({worst_lag_fuel}). <strong>{stale_pct_overall:.1f}%</strong> of records are more than one week stale. For the Fuel Finder scheme to serve its consumer transparency purpose, mandating a maximum reporting interval (e.g., daily or every 48 hours) could significantly improve data quality.
            </li>
